\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{A causality sign is not a reflection}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{The Lorentzian star of
\citet{Definition 12.4, p. 42}{desbrunhiranileokmarsden2005dec}
multiplies the Euclidean volume ratio by a sign #{\kappa}
declared on each cell: #{+1} when every edge of the cell is
spacelike, and #{-1} otherwise. A vertex has no edge, so its
sign is #{+1}. That sign is data on the cell. The twisted
form of \ref{fdip-00Q1} is not read off such a sign. The
case with no reflection, and a negative weight, is
\ref{fdip-00Q3}, and the kinematic character of the
signature is \ref{fdip-00Q4}.}
